# Docstrings of the dynamic model structs.
#
# Several model files are generated by a DSL compiler and must not be
# edited by hand, so the struct docstrings live here and are attached with `@doc`.
# Included after all model files.

@doc """
    GENCLS{T} <: AbstractModel

Classical synchronous machine: a constant internal voltage `E_q` behind the transient
impedance `r_a + j xd_p`, with the swing equation for the rotor.

A GENCLS record replaces the power flow generator (PV or Slack) with the same bus
number and ID. `xd_p` and `r_a` are inherited from that generator's source impedance
(`zx`, `zr`); the DYR record supplies `H` and `D`.

# Parameters
- `BUS`: bus number of the generator
- `ID`: generator ID (up to two characters)
- `H`: inertia constant (s, MW·s/MVA)
- `D`: damping coefficient (pu)
- `xd_p`: d-axis transient reactance (pu)
- `r_a`: armature resistance (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Equations
- `dδ/dt = ω_b (ω - 1)`, with `ω_b` the system base angular frequency
- `2H dω/dt = P_m - P_e - D (ω - 1)`

# Variables
- States: `delta` (rotor angle, rad), `omega` (rotor speed, pu)
- Algebraic: `E_q` (internal voltage, receives the exciter contribution), `P_m`
  (mechanical power, receives the governor contribution)
- Observables: `P_e`, `Q_e` (electrical power output)

Without an exciter or governor, `E_q` and `P_m` stay at their initial values.
Initialization computes `delta`, `E_q` and `P_m` from the power flow voltage and the
replaced generator's output, with `omega = 1`.
""" GENCLS

@doc """
    GENROU{T} <: AbstractModel

Round rotor synchronous machine with quadratic saturation: two rotor circuits on each
axis (transient and subtransient), with equal d- and q-axis subtransient reactances
(`X''q = X''d`).

A GENROU record replaces the power flow generator (PV or Slack) with the same bus
number and ID. The armature resistance `ra` is inherited from that generator's source
resistance (`zr`).

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and generator ID
- `Td10`, `Td20`: d-axis transient and subtransient open-circuit time constants (s)
- `Tq10`, `Tq20`: q-axis transient and subtransient open-circuit time constants (s)
- `H`: inertia constant (s); `D`: damping coefficient (pu)
- `Xd`, `Xq`: d- and q-axis synchronous reactances (pu)
- `Xd1`, `Xq1`: d- and q-axis transient reactances (pu)
- `Xd2`: subtransient reactance (pu), used on both axes
- `Xl`: leakage reactance (pu)
- `S10`, `S12`: saturation factors at 1.0 and 1.2 pu flux; the quadratic
  saturation function acts on the subtransient air-gap flux magnitude
- `ra`: armature resistance (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `delta` (rotor angle, rad), `omega` (rotor speed, pu), `e1q`, `e1d`
  (q- and d-axis transient voltages), `e2d`, `e2q` (d- and q-axis subtransient
  voltages)
- Algebraic: `Id`, `Iq` (armature currents in the rotor frame), `E_fd` (field
  voltage, receives the exciter contribution), `P_m` (mechanical power, receives the
  governor contribution)
- Observables: `Pe`, `Qe` (electrical power output)

The swing equation is `2H dω/dt = P_m - τ_e - D (ω - 1)` with the air-gap torque
`τ_e`. Without an exciter or governor, `E_fd` and `P_m` stay at their initial values.
""" GENROU

@doc """
    GENSAL{T} <: AbstractModel

Salient pole synchronous machine with quadratic saturation on the d axis: a field and
a damper circuit on the d axis and one subtransient circuit on the q axis.

A GENSAL record replaces the power flow generator (PV or Slack) with the same bus
number and ID. The armature resistance `ra` is inherited from that generator's source
resistance (`zr`).

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and generator ID
- `Td10`, `Td20`: d-axis transient and subtransient open-circuit time constants (s)
- `Tq20`: q-axis subtransient open-circuit time constant (s)
- `H`: inertia constant (s); `D`: damping coefficient (pu)
- `Xd`, `Xq`: d- and q-axis synchronous reactances (pu)
- `Xd1`: d-axis transient reactance (pu)
- `Xd2`: d-axis subtransient reactance (pu); the q-axis one is not a parameter: Xq'' = Xd'' (PSS/E GENSAL)
- `Xl`: leakage reactance (pu)
- `S10`, `S12`: saturation factors at 1.0 and 1.2 pu flux; the quadratic
  saturation function acts on `e1q`
- `ra`: armature resistance (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `delta` (rotor angle, rad), `omega` (rotor speed, pu), `e1q` (q-axis
  transient voltage), `psi1d` (d-axis damper flux), `psi2q` (q-axis subtransient
  flux)
- Algebraic: `Id`, `Iq` (armature currents in the rotor frame), `E_fd` (field
  voltage, receives the exciter contribution), `P_m` (mechanical power, receives the
  governor contribution)
- Observables: `Pe`, `Qe` (electrical power output)
""" GENSAL

@doc """
    GENTPJ1{T} <: AbstractModel

Round rotor synchronous machine (GENTPJ1): the [`GENROU`](@ref) structure with separate
d- and q-axis subtransient reactances and a saturation input that includes the armature
current, `ψ'' + Kis |I|`. q-axis saturation is the d-axis value scaled by `Xq/Xd`.

A GENTPJ1 record replaces the power flow generator (PV or Slack) with the same bus
number and ID. The armature resistance `ra` is inherited from that generator's source
resistance (`zr`).

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and generator ID
- `Td10`, `Td20`: d-axis transient and subtransient open-circuit time constants (s)
- `Tq10`, `Tq20`: q-axis transient and subtransient open-circuit time constants (s)
- `H`: inertia constant (s); `D`: damping coefficient (pu)
- `Xd`, `Xq`: d- and q-axis synchronous reactances (pu)
- `Xd1`, `Xq1`: d- and q-axis transient reactances (pu)
- `Xd2`, `Xq2`: d- and q-axis subtransient reactances (pu)
- `Xl`: leakage reactance (pu)
- `S10`, `S12`: saturation factors at 1.0 and 1.2 pu flux
- `Kis`: armature current multiplier in the saturation input
- `ra`: armature resistance (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `delta`, `omega`, `e1q`, `e1d`, `e2d`, `e2q` (as in [`GENROU`](@ref))
- Algebraic: `Id`, `Iq`, `E_fd`, `P_m`
- Observables: `Pe`, `Qe`
""" GENTPJ1

@doc """
    TGOV1{T} <: AbstractModel

Steam turbine governor (TGOV1). Controls the mechanical power `P_m` of the generator
with the same bus number and ID (any model of the `:generators` group).

The speed deviation `ω - 1` is subtracted from the power reference `pref` and scaled by
`1/R`; a first-order lag `1/(1 + s T1)` with anti-windup limits `[Vmin, Vmax]` gives
the valve position; a lead-lag `(1 + s T2)/(1 + s T3)` gives the turbine power, from
which the damping term `Dt (ω - 1)` is subtracted. The result, `pout`, is the
generator's mechanical power.

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and ID of the controlled generator
- `R`: speed droop (pu)
- `T1`: governor lag time constant (s)
- `T2`: lead time constant (s)
- `T3`: lag time constant (s)
- `Dt`: turbine damping coefficient (pu)
- `Vmax`, `Vmin`: valve position limits (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `LAG_y` (valve position), `LL_x` (lead-lag state)
- Algebraic: `pref` (power reference, held at its initial value), `pout` (turbine
  output power)
- External: `omega` (speed of the controlled generator)

Initialization sets `pout` to the generator's initial mechanical power and the reference
to `R` times it, which holds the governor at rest at synchronous speed: it reads the
generator's initial speed and refuses a start at any other.
""" TGOV1

@doc """
    SEXS{T} <: AbstractModel

Simplified excitation system (SEXS). Controls the field voltage `E_fd` of the
generator with the same bus number and ID (any model of the `:generators` group).

The voltage error `vref - v` (with `v` the terminal bus voltage magnitude) passes
through a lead-lag `(1 + s TA)/(1 + s TB)`, with `TA = TATB · TB`, and a lag
`K/(1 + s TE)` with anti-windup limits `[EMIN, EMAX]`, whose output is `E_fd`.

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and ID of the controlled generator
- `TATB`: ratio `TA/TB` of the lead-lag
- `TB`: lag time constant of the lead-lag (s)
- `K`: gain (pu)
- `TE`: exciter time constant (s)
- `EMIN`, `EMAX`: field voltage limits (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `LL_x` (lead-lag state), `LAW_y` (exciter output)
- Algebraic: `vref` (voltage reference, held at its initial value), `E_fd` (field
  voltage)
- External: `v` (terminal bus voltage magnitude)

Initialization sets `E_fd` to the generator's initial field voltage and
`vref = v + E_fd/K`.
""" SEXS

@doc """
    IEEEX1{T} <: AbstractModel

IEEE Type 1 excitation system (IEEEX1) with a transient gain reduction. Controls the
field voltage `E_fd` of the generator with the same bus number and ID (any model of the
`:generators` group).

The terminal voltage passes through the transducer `1/(1 + s TR)`; the error `vref - vm - vf`
(`vf` the rate feedback) passes through the lead-lag `(1 + s TC)/(1 + s TB)` and the
regulator `KA/(1 + s TA)`, whose state is held within `[VRMIN, VRMAX]`. The exciter
`TE dE_fd/dt = VR - (KE E_fd + E_fd SE(E_fd))` has no limit, `SE` being the quadratic
saturation through `(E1, SE1)` and `(E2, SE2)`, and the rate feedback is
`vf = KF s/(1 + s TF) E_fd`. `TR = 0` is the direct measurement, `TA = 0` the clamped gain,
and `TB = TC = 0` no lead-lag.

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and ID of the controlled generator
- `TR`, `TA`, `TB`, `TC`, `TE`, `TF`: time constants (s)
- `KA`, `KE`: regulator gain and exciter self-excitation constant (pu); `KF`: feedback gain (s)
- `VRMAX`, `VRMIN`: regulator output limits (pu)
- `Switch`: PSS/E record field the diagram does not draw; only 0 is accepted
- `E1`, `SE1`, `E2`, `SE2`: the two points of the saturation curve
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `LG_x` (transducer), `LL_x` (lead-lag), `VR_x` (regulator), `EFD_y` (exciter output),
  `WF_x` (rate feedback washout)
- Algebraic: `vref` (voltage reference, held at its initial value), `yfb` (rate feedback),
  `E_fd` (field voltage)
- External: `v` (terminal bus voltage magnitude)

Initialization sets `E_fd` to the generator's initial field voltage and
`vref = v + VR0/KA`, with `VR0 = KE E_fd + E_fd SE(E_fd)` the regulator output the exciter
needs at rest.
""" IEEEX1

@doc """
    REGCA1{T} <: AbstractREGCA1

Renewable energy generator/converter model A (REGC_A, WECC 2014, section 3.2 and
Appendix A), generated: a current source that turns the active and reactive
current commands `Ipcmd` and `Iqcmd` into the power it delivers at its terminal, `Pe` and
`Qe`. Used for wind and solar plants, driven by an electrical controller such as
[`REECA1`](@ref), which measures `Pe` and `Qe`.

A REGCA1 record replaces the power flow generator (PV or Slack) with the same bus
number and ID. All parameters come from the DYR record; the data is checked where it
enters (its divisors nonzero, and `Zerox < Brkpt`, `Lvpnt0 < Lvpnt1`, `Rrpwr > 0`,
`Iqrmax > 0`, `Iqrmin < 0`, `Iolim < 0`).

The record is per unit on the converter's own MVA base `Mbase`, which is the `MBASE` of
the generator it replaces: it is not a field of the model or of the record, and is
carried by the system as the rating of the linked device (`ModelRating(:mbase,
NominalPower; from = LinkedDevice())`). The states, `Ipcmd`, `Iqcmd`, `Pe` and `Qe` are
on that base; the model converts to the system base `Sbase` only where it meets the
network, with the derived ratio `Mratio = Mbase/Sbase` (`RatingRatio`), in its bus
injections and in the division of the power flow's powers at the start.

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and generator ID
- `Lvplsw`: low voltage power logic (LVPL) switch, 1 = LVPL acts, 0 = it does not
- `Tg`: converter time constant of both current lags (s)
- `Rrpwr`: up-ramp rate limit of the active current (pu/s)
- `Brkpt`, `Zerox`: LVPL breakpoint and zero crossing (pu)
- `Lvpl1`: LVPL at `Brkpt` (pu)
- `Volim`: the voltage the high-voltage reactive current management keeps `Vt` at or
  below (pu)
- `Lvpnt1`, `Lvpnt0`: voltages of the low-voltage active current management, which
  passes all of the active current above `Lvpnt1` and none below `Lvpnt0` (pu)
- `Iolim`: the floor of the reactive power of the high-voltage management (pu)
- `Tfltr`: time constant of the voltage filter that LVPL reads (s)
- `Khv`, `Accel`: acceleration factors of an iterative network solution; read from the
  DYR record, with no effect on the simultaneous solution
- `Iqrmax`, `Iqrmin`: rate limits of the reactive current (pu/s), the first acting where
  the initial reactive power is positive, the second where it is negative
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `Vflt` (filtered voltage), `Ip` (active current, its rise limited at `Rrpwr`,
  within LVPL), `Iq_lag` (reactive current, positive injecting)
- Algebraic: `Ipcmd`, `Iqcmd` (current commands, held at their initial values plus the
  controller's contribution), `dq` (the reduction of the reactive power by the
  high-voltage management), `Pe`, `Qe` (the power injected into the bus, on the
  converter's base)
- External: `v` (terminal voltage magnitude)

`Pe = Ip · multp · v` and `Qe = max(Iq_lag · v - dq, Iolim)`, `multp` the low-voltage
active current management. Its discrete behaviour is automata, whose transitions the
simulation locates: `iq_recovery` (which rate limit of `Iq_lag` acts, chosen by the sign
of the initial reactive power `Qgen0`), `hv` (the high-voltage management: `dq` holds
`Vt` at `Volim` while the reactive power is above its floor) and `Ip` (the active
current: free, rise-limited at `Rrpwr`, or held within LVPL, a non-windup upper limit
that falls with the filtered voltage and carries `Ip` down with it). `Iq_lag` follows `-Iqcmd`, so a positive `Iqcmd` absorbs
reactive power. A start the model does not have is refused: active power at or below
`Lvpnt0` other than zero, reactive power below `Iolim`, a voltage above `Volim` with the
reactive power above its floor, or an active current above LVPL.
""" REGCA1

@doc """
    AbstractREGCA1 <: AbstractModel

The family of the renewable energy generator/converter model A: [`REGCA1`](@ref) and the types that share its physics.
The traits that follow from the physics (the positive-sequence phase representation, the role of a current source in the
Y-bus simulation, the converter's current limit through its [`REECA1`](@ref)) are declared on the family.
""" AbstractREGCA1

@doc """
    REGFM_A1{T} <: AbstractModel

Droop-controlled grid-forming converter (WECC REGFM_A1, PNNL-35110), generated: a
voltage source behind the coupling reactance `XL` whose frequency follows the
active power by the P-f droop `mp` and whose voltage follows the reactive power by the
Q-V droop `mq`, with the voltage controller, the overload mitigation of the active power
and the Qmax/Qmin controller of the reactive power. It belongs to the `:ren_gfm` group.

A REGFM_A1 record replaces the power flow generator (PV or Slack) with the same bus
number and ID. The data is checked where it enters (flags 0 or 1, ordered limits,
positive `XL`, `ImaxF` and integral gains, nonzero filter time constants).

The record is per unit on the converter's own MVA base `Mbase`, the `MBASE` of the
generator it replaces, as REGCA1's is: it has no rating field, and the system carries the
rating of the linked device and the derived ratio `Mratio = Mbase/Sbase`. The states,
references, `Pe` and `Qe` are on the converter's base, and `Mratio` converts the bus
injections and the power flow's powers at the start.

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and converter ID
- `XL`: coupling reactance (pu)
- `mq`: Q-V droop gain
- `kpv`, `kiv`: gains of the voltage controller (`VFlag = 1`)
- `Emax`, `Emin`: limits of the output of the voltage loop
- `mp`: P-f droop gain
- `Pmax`, `Pmin`: limits of the active power output (`Pmin` negative for storage)
- `kppmax`, `kipmax`: gains of the overload mitigation controller
- `w0`: rated angular frequency (rad/s)
- `TPf`, `TQf`, `TVf`: time constants of the power and voltage measurement filters (s)
- `Qmax`, `Qmin`: limits of the reactive power output
- `VFlag`: 1 regulates the terminal voltage through the PI, 0 the internal voltage directly
- `QVFlag`: 1 the plant controller changes `Vref`, 0 it changes `Qref`
- `kpqmax`, `kiqmax`: gains of the Qmax and Qmin controller
- `ImaxF`: maximum transient output current (pu)
- `status`: `true` when in service

The record's fields follow the order of PNNL-35110 Table 1.

# Variables
- States: `Pinv`, `Qinv`, `Vinv` (filtered power and voltage), `delta` (angle of the
  internal voltage), `xpmax`, `xpmin`, `xqmax`, `xqmin` (limit controllers' integrators),
  `xv` (integrator of the voltage controller)
- Algebraic: `Pref`, `Qref`, `Vref` (references the plant controller changes), `Vcmd`,
  `Edroop`, `Idroop`, `Pe`, `Qe`
- External: `v`, `theta` (terminal voltage)

The integrators' limits, the voltage controller's, and the current limit are automata,
whose transitions the simulation locates.
""" REGFM_A1

@doc """
    REECA1{T} <: AbstractModel

Renewable energy electrical control model A (REEC_A, WECC 2014), generated.
Computes the current commands `Ipcmd` and `Iqcmd` of the converter model with the same
bus number and ID (any model of the `:ren_gen` group, such as [`REGCA1`](@ref)) from
active and reactive power references, the terminal voltage, and the electrical and
reactive power the converter delivers (its `Pe` and `Qe`, measured as REECA1's `Pe` and
`Qgen`).

Features: reactive power or power factor reference (`PFFLAG`), reactive power PI
control (`VFLAG`), voltage PI control (`QFLAG`), non-windup PI controllers, the voltage
dip state machine (the reactive current injection `Kqv · (voltage error)` outside the
deadband `[dbd1, dbd2]` during a dip, the controllers and the power order frozen, and
the `Thld` and `Thld2` holds after it), rate-limited active power reference, the power
order within `[PMIN, PMAX]` with anti-windup, voltage-dependent current limits (VDL1 and
VDL2) and P or Q priority current limiting (`PQFLAG`). The limits, the dip logic and the
holds are automata, whose transitions the simulation locates.

# Parameters
- `BUS`, `ID`: bus number and ID of the controlled converter model
- `VREG`: remote voltage regulation bus; stored, the terminal bus voltage is used
- Flags (each 0 or 1, of the parameters' element type): `PFFLAG` (1 = power factor
  control, 0 = Q control), `VFLAG` (1 = Q control, 0 = voltage control), `QFLAG` (1 =
  voltage or Q control, 0 = constant power factor or Q), `PFLAG` (1 = speed dependent
  active power reference), `PQFLAG` (0 = Q priority, 1 = P priority)
- Voltage dip: `Vdip`, `Vup` (dip thresholds, pu), `Trv` (voltage filter time
  constant, s), `dbd1`, `dbd2` (deadband), `Kqv` (reactive current injection gain),
  `Iqh1`, `Iql1` (injection limits), `Vref0` (voltage reference; 0 uses the initial
  filtered voltage)
- After a dip: `Thld` (the injection is held at `Iqfrz` for `Thld` s if `Thld > 0`,
  continues for `-Thld` s if `Thld < 0`, ends at once if 0), `Thld2` (time `Ipmax` is
  held at its dip value, s)
- Reactive power control: `Tp` (electrical power filter time constant, s), `QMax`,
  `QMin` (reactive power limits), `VMAX`, `VMIN` (voltage control limits), `Kqp`,
  `Kqi` (reactive power PI gains), `Kvp`, `Kvi` (voltage PI gains), `Vref1` (voltage
  reference when `VFLAG = 0`), `Tiq` (reactive current filter time constant, s)
- Active power control: `dPmax`, `dPmin` (power reference rate limits, pu/s), `PMAX`,
  `PMIN` (power order limits), `Imax` (apparent current limit), `Tpord` (power order
  time constant, s)
- VDL1 (reactive current limit versus voltage): points `(Vq1, Iq1)` to `(Vq4, Iq4)`
- VDL2 (active current limit versus voltage): points `(Vp1, Ip1)` to `(Vp4, Ip4)`
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Variables
- States: `s0_y` (filtered voltage), `S1_y` (filtered electrical power), `PIQ_xi`,
  `PIV_xi` (PI integrators), `s4_y` (reactive current from the power factor path),
  `pfilt_y` (filtered power reference), `s5_y` (power order)
- Algebraic: `Pref`, `Qref`, `pfaref` (references, held at their initial values),
  `Qerr` (reactive power error), `Vsel` (voltage reference of the voltage PI),
  `Iqinj` (reactive current injection of the dip logic), `Iqmax`, `Ipmax` (current
  limits; `Iqmin = -Iqmax`, `Ipmin = 0`), `Ipcmd_out`, `Iqcmd_out` (current commands)
- External: `v` (terminal voltage magnitude), `Pe` and `Qgen` (the active and reactive
  power the converter delivers, its `Pe` and `Qe`)

`Iqcmd_out` follows the specification's sign: positive injects reactive power. REECA1
delivers `-(Iqcmd_out - Iqcmd0)` to the converter's `Iqcmd`, whose positive value absorbs.
The power reference filter time constant is fixed at 0.02 s, and the speed used by
the speed-dependent active power path is 1 pu.

The start is at rest: where the voltage PI is used (`QFLAG = 1`) the voltage, and where
the Q PI is used (`VFLAG = 1`) `Qgen`, is within that controller's limits, unless the start
is in a dip (where the controllers are frozen); a start outside them is refused.
""" REECA1

@doc """
    VoltagePlayback{T} <: AbstractModel

A bus driven by a recorded voltage: it holds the magnitude and angle of its bus to
recorded series `V(t)` and `Θ(t)` and supplies whatever power that takes, so that the
devices at the bus can be simulated against measurements (playback validation).

It replaces the power flow Slack unit with the same bus number and ID, as a dynamic
generator replaces its Slack or PV unit. Every Slack unit at its bus must be replaced
(by it or by the dynamic models of the other units); a unit left in place would hold
the bus voltage too. It has no DYR record: build it with
`VoltagePlayback(bus; id, t, v, theta, breaks)` and add it to the dynamic models,
`merge(dyr, (VoltagePlayback = StructArray([pb]),))`.

# Parameters
- `BUS`: bus number
- `ID`: the ID of the Slack unit it replaces (default `"1"`)
- `rec`: the recording, a [`PlaybackRecording`](@ref): samples `t` (s), `v` (pu),
  `theta` (rad) and `breaks` (s), interpolated by a not-a-knot cubic spline in each
  piece between breaks
- `piece`: the piece of the recording (between breaks) the playback is on; the problem
  sets it at the start and the breaks advance it
- `status`: `true` when in service

# Equations
- `0 = v - V(t)`, `0 = θ - Θ(t)` (the bus voltage is the recorded one)
- `p` and `q` enter the bus power balance as injections

# Variables
- Algebraic: `p`, `q` (the active and reactive power injected into the bus, pu on the
  system base); they start at the power of the replaced Slack unit in the power flow
- External: `v`, `theta` (the bus voltage)

# Breaks
`breaks` are the times where the recording is discontinuous (a fault applied or
cleared). The interpolant restarts at each, and the simulation stops there: the step
before ends on the value before the break, a reinitialization of the algebraic variables
takes the value after it. They are the playback's only events.

# The start
At `tspan[1]` the power flow's voltage at the bus must be the recording's (after a
break at `tspan[1]`), to the power flow's residual: set the Slack's `vs` and the bus's
`va` to [`playback_voltage`](@ref)`(pb, tspan[1])`. The problem refuses a start that
differs, and a `tspan` outside the recording.

The model has no parameters of its own, and runs on the CPU only.
""" VoltagePlayback
